[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Divergences found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two real defects were found by these tests and fixed in `src/lib.rs` (the C was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m never touched):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`c2Dot` NaN payload (rows 9/11, `regression_nan_order.rs`).** The C's final
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `addss` takes the **y term** as `src1`, and its y-term `mulss` takes **`b.y`**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    as `src1`. x86 forwards `src1` quieted when it is NaN, so a naive
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y` returned the *x* term's payload/sign instead. Observed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    at 126 mismatches per 2 M random bit patterns; now 0. Fixed by modelling the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    x86 operand selection in `mul_ss` / `add_ss`, because LLVM commutes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `fadd`/`fmul` freely and Rust source order cannot pin it down.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **`c2Collided` unaligned loads (rows 12 / `err_row12`, debug profile only).**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `*(c2Circle *)A` in C compiles to alignment-agnostic x86 loads, so a caller may
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    pass an unaligned pointer. The Rust `*(A as *const c2Circle)` tripped the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    debug-assertions "misaligned pointer dereference" check and aborted. Fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    with `core::ptr::read_unaligned`. This one was invisible in the release
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    profile — it is why the suite runs under **both** cargo profiles.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Note on NaN payloads and the C build configuration
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, so the documented build
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`cmake .. -DCMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON && cmake --build .`) compiles at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-O0`, and that `.so` is the ground truth these tests load. gcc's choice of SSE
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `src1` operand is **not stable across `-O` levels** — measured on this toolchain:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | build | `c2Dot` y-term `src1` | `c2Dot` add `src1` | `c2Mulvs` `src1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |-------|----------------------|--------------------|------------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-O0` (the documented build) | `b.y` | y term | vector component |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-O1` / `-O2` / `-Os` | `a.y` | x term | vector component |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-O3` (auto-vectorised `mulps`) | `a.y` | x term | **scalar** |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No single translation can match all of these simultaneously, so the Rust matches
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-O0`, i.e. the build the task specifies. If the C is ever rebuilt at another
