The C is [38;5;10mreturn a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y;[0m, which gcc at [38;5;10m-O0[0m lowers to SSE scalar ops. On x86, [38;5;10mOPss dst, src[0m with a NaN operand [3mforwards[23m [38;5;10msrc1[0m (quieted) rather than computing — so operand order is observable in the returned float. gcc's order is asymmetric: the y-term [38;5;10mmulss[0m takes [38;5;10mb.y[0m as [38;5;10msrc1[0m, and the final [38;5;10maddss[0m takes the [1my term[22m as [38;5;10msrc1[0m. The naive Rust returned the x term's payload and sign instead. Reordering the source doesn't help, because LLVM's register allocator commutes [38;5;10mfadd[0m/[38;5;10mfmul[0m freely — I verified that by disassembling. Fixed by modelling the x86 selection rule explicitly in [38;5;10mmul_ss[0m / [38;5;10madd_ss[0m; non-NaN paths still defer to the hardware, so ordinary results are bit-identical by construction.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
